  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A slow moving cold front approaches and moves through the\n  area on Friday, bringing the potential for heavy rain to\n  portions of the area beginning on Thursday.\n\nUpper level troughing pivoting over the Great Lakes region\nslowly tracks eastward through Friday, before the upper low\ntracks overhead on Saturday. At the surface, this translates a\nslow moving cold front approaching the region on Thursday,\nbefore slowly moving overhead and stalling nearby through\nFriday.\n\nAs the front approaches from the west, precipitation chances\nincrease for those in the western portions of the forecast area\nthroughout the day on Thursday. Those along and east of the\nmetros will stay primarily dry with 30% chance of precipitation\nor less. Precipitation chances increase Thursday night into\nFriday as the cold front approaches and moisture increases.\nBeneficial rainfall is expected with REFS ensembles showing a 24\nhour precipitation total swath stretching from southern \nDelaware across the New Jersey coast of 1 to 1.5 inches. \nFurther west, precipitation totals range from a trace to an \ninch. Exactly where the heaviest precipitation totals line up \nremains uncertain and will be dependent on where the front \nstalls. Overall, expected precipitation, heavy for some, to \nreturn to the forecast this weekend. \n\nThe cold front departs offshore on Saturday, with dry conditions\nreturning to the forecast. High temperatures will be noticeably\ncooler with highs in the upper 50s to mid 60s. Overnight low \ntemperatures will be in the 40s for most. Temperatures continue \ncooling Sunday into early next week with highs in the upper 50s \nto low 60s and overnight lows in the 30s for most (40s metro\nareas).",
